1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,986
Five more minutes. Pleeease. Exam's at nine.

2
00:00:03,986 --> 00:00:06,533
Fine. I'll stop the sunrise.

3
00:00:06,899 --> 00:00:09,929
In one second, the ground under you stops.

4
00:00:09,929 --> 00:00:11,179
You don't.

5
00:00:11,179 --> 00:00:15,409
You keep flying east… at over 1,600 km/h.

6
00:00:17,049 --> 00:00:18,743
…Nova. What did you DO?

7
00:00:18,743 --> 00:00:22,390
I stopped the Earth spinning. You wanted more sleep.

8
00:00:22,390 --> 00:00:24,412
Stick with me to the end.

9
00:00:24,412 --> 00:00:29,424
I'll show you the one thing in your night sky that would start this whole planet spinning again.

10
00:00:29,424 --> 00:00:31,853
And what it would cost us.

11
00:00:32,466 --> 00:00:34,015
What do you mean, "cost"?

12
00:00:34,015 --> 00:00:37,386
Let's rewind. One second before the brake.

13
00:00:43,322 --> 00:00:45,908
Right now, you think you're sitting still.

14
00:00:45,908 --> 00:00:47,420
Nope. Wrong.

15
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:51,598
The Earth turns once every 23 hours and 56 minutes.

16
00:00:51,748 --> 00:00:56,733
At the equator, the ground moves at 1,670 km/h.

17
00:00:56,733 --> 00:00:58,504
That's faster than a plane!

18
00:00:58,504 --> 00:01:00,476
Faster than sound.

19
00:01:00,826 --> 00:01:09,282
In Chennai or Bengaluru, you're doing about 1,600. Mumbai, around 1,580. Delhi, about 1,465.

20
00:01:09,282 --> 00:01:14,941
At the North Pole? Almost zero. You just turn slowly. Like a cake in a microwave.

21
00:01:14,941 --> 00:01:18,299
Then why don't I feel it? Why isn't my chai flying off the table?

22
00:01:18,299 --> 00:01:21,819
Hold that thought. First, why is it spinning at all?

23
00:01:21,819 --> 00:01:27,058
Four and a half billion years ago, Earth formed from a spinning cloud of gas and dust.

24
00:01:27,058 --> 00:01:29,605
And that spin never stopped.

25
00:01:29,757 --> 00:01:34,657
In space, there's almost nothing to slow it down. No air. No brakes.

26
00:01:34,838 --> 00:01:38,609
And you don't feel it for the same reason you don't feel a smooth flight.

27
00:01:38,609 --> 00:01:43,023
Your body only feels changes in speed. Not speed itself.

28
00:01:43,273 --> 00:01:48,079
Your chai is moving at 1,600 too. You're all moving together.

29
00:01:48,129 --> 00:01:50,163
So my chai is… a rocket.

30
00:01:50,163 --> 00:01:52,945
Your chai is a very calm rocket.

31
00:01:53,395 --> 00:01:57,644
Here's something weird. All that spinning makes Earth fat in the middle.

32
00:01:57,644 --> 00:02:01,223
The equator bulges out about 21 kilometres.

33
00:02:01,473 --> 00:02:05,708
So the point on Earth farthest from its centre… isn't Everest.

34
00:02:05,708 --> 00:02:10,264
It's Mount Chimborazo, in Ecuador. Two kilometres farther out.

35
00:02:10,614 --> 00:02:13,263
Everest is going to be so upset.

36
00:02:13,263 --> 00:02:18,754
And the spin is slowing down. Just a little. The Moon's tides act like a gentle brake.

37
00:02:18,754 --> 00:02:22,764
Every century, the day gets about 2 milliseconds longer.

38
00:02:22,764 --> 00:02:24,790
So I'll get more sleep eventually!

39
00:02:24,790 --> 00:02:29,294
In about 17 million years, you'll get your five extra minutes.

40
00:02:29,294 --> 00:02:31,264
…I can't wait that long.

41
00:02:31,264 --> 00:02:33,783
Which is why I built the Spin Brake.

42
00:02:34,023 --> 00:02:39,511
But before I pull it again… where on Earth would YOU survive the first second?

43
00:02:40,994 --> 00:02:42,745
Okay. Rules.

44
00:02:42,745 --> 00:02:47,905
Quick note: nothing in nature can actually do this. It's a thought experiment.

45
00:02:47,905 --> 00:02:52,592
The solid Earth stops in one second. Rock. Mountains. Buildings.

46
00:02:52,592 --> 00:02:56,270
Everything else — the air, the water, the people — keeps going.

47
00:02:56,270 --> 00:02:57,209
Ready?

48
00:02:57,209 --> 00:02:58,659
No.

49
00:02:58,659 --> 00:03:02,664
Stopping from 1,600 km/h in one second…

50
00:03:02,664 --> 00:03:05,471
…is a push of about 47 g.

51
00:03:05,621 --> 00:03:08,462
Jet pilots black out at around 9.

52
00:03:08,883 --> 00:03:10,781
So… 47 is bad.

53
00:03:10,781 --> 00:03:13,975
Forty-seven is "you are now a pancake."

54
00:03:14,179 --> 00:03:20,020
Anything bolted down — buildings, bridges, trees — gets that jolt. Most of it snaps.

55
00:03:20,061 --> 00:03:22,519
And anything not bolted down…

56
00:03:22,519 --> 00:03:25,419
…flies east. Faster than a bullet.

57
00:03:25,444 --> 00:03:27,724
People. Cars. Cows. Your chai.

58
00:03:27,724 --> 00:03:29,480
NOT THE CHAI!

59
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:35,674
And that's not even the worst part. There's a second killer. It's coming in a few minutes.

60
00:03:35,810 --> 00:03:38,134
So where would you survive?

61
00:03:38,134 --> 00:03:41,308
Remember the North Pole, spinning at almost zero?

62
00:03:41,308 --> 00:03:43,920
There, the stop is barely a nudge.

63
00:03:43,920 --> 00:03:47,460
The poles are the safest place on Earth… for now.

64
00:03:47,460 --> 00:03:50,034
Booking a ticket to Antarctica.

65
00:03:50,034 --> 00:03:52,918
Now. How much energy did I just delete?

66
00:03:52,918 --> 00:03:59,097
Earth's spin holds as much energy as the Sun gives out — in total — in nine whole minutes.

67
00:03:59,097 --> 00:04:02,720
The entire Sun. In every direction. Nine minutes.

68
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:06,593
That's about half a million dinosaur-killing asteroids.

69
00:04:06,593 --> 00:04:08,957
And you used it… so I could sleep in.

70
00:04:08,957 --> 00:04:10,757
You're welcome.

71
00:04:11,790 --> 00:04:13,590
Hour one.

72
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:20,152
Here's the second killer. The air itself.

73
00:04:20,152 --> 00:04:25,498
The atmosphere was never glued to the ground. It's still moving east at full speed.

74
00:04:25,498 --> 00:04:30,455
So on the ground, it feels like a wind of up to 1,600 km/h.

75
00:04:30,455 --> 00:04:35,507
Faster than sound. A supersonic storm across almost all of India.

76
00:04:35,657 --> 00:04:37,989
How bad is that compared to a cyclone?

77
00:04:37,989 --> 00:04:43,555
The strongest cyclones reach about 300. This is more than four times that.

78
00:04:43,705 --> 00:04:47,605
Topsoil. Trees. Houses. Scraped clean off the land.

79
00:04:51,066 --> 00:04:54,930
And all that air rubbing against the ground turns into heat.

80
00:04:54,930 --> 00:05:00,901
Fun fact: after today, you'll never see another cyclone. Ever. I'll tell you why soon.

81
00:05:04,751 --> 00:05:11,065
The moving air alone carries about as much energy as the asteroid that wiped out the dinosaurs.

82
00:05:11,065 --> 00:05:12,332
Just the AIR?

83
00:05:12,332 --> 00:05:16,058
Just the air. The oceans carry hundreds of times more.

84
00:05:16,058 --> 00:05:20,527
The oceans don't stop either. They slam east into the continents.

85
00:05:20,527 --> 00:05:26,003
The Arabian Sea charges straight at Mumbai. Waves bigger than any tsunami in history.

86
00:05:27,343 --> 00:05:33,162
And here's the strange part. Even after the waves calm down… the water won't stay put.

87
00:05:33,162 --> 00:05:36,121
It starts to drain away. Somewhere.

88
00:05:37,815 --> 00:05:41,805
It's day one. The winds are finally dying down. Look up.

89
00:05:41,805 --> 00:05:43,999
The Sun… isn't moving.

90
00:05:44,583 --> 00:05:51,189
It was sunrise in India when I pulled the brake. It's still sunrise. Tomorrow too. And next week.

91
00:05:51,416 --> 00:05:57,135
Now, the cyclones. They spin because the Earth spins. It's called the Coriolis effect.

92
00:05:57,135 --> 00:06:02,800
That's why cyclones almost never form right on the equator, where that effect is weakest.

93
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:05,392
No spin. No spinning storms.

94
00:06:05,542 --> 00:06:11,324
Instead, one giant wind blows from the scorching day side to the frozen night side.

95
00:06:11,410 --> 00:06:13,742
Well, at least I can watch the cricket match.

96
00:06:13,742 --> 00:06:15,348
Not on your dish.

97
00:06:15,348 --> 00:06:20,691
TV satellites circle the Earth once a day, so they stay above the same spot.

98
00:06:20,691 --> 00:06:27,656
Now the Earth isn't turning. The satellite keeps going. It slides across the sky, and your signal goes with it.

99
00:06:27,656 --> 00:06:29,497
NOOO! LAST OVER!

100
00:06:29,539 --> 00:06:32,194
Now, the water. Remember the fat middle?

101
00:06:32,194 --> 00:06:36,851
The spin was flinging the oceans outward, holding them up around the equator.

102
00:06:36,851 --> 00:06:40,520
Take the spin away, and the equator becomes a giant hill.

103
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:42,825
And water runs downhill.

104
00:06:42,825 --> 00:06:45,196
All the way to the poles.

105
00:06:50,522 --> 00:06:53,122
Okay, but the Sun will set eventually. Right?

106
00:06:53,122 --> 00:06:56,610
Eventually. And when the next sunrise comes…

107
00:06:59,511 --> 00:07:01,668
It rises in the west.

108
00:07:03,018 --> 00:07:08,585
Earth still goes around the Sun, so the Sun crawls slowly backwards across the sky.

109
00:07:08,585 --> 00:07:11,544
One sunrise. Per year. In the west.

110
00:07:13,945 --> 00:07:17,139
Year one. A day now lasts a whole year.

111
00:07:17,722 --> 00:07:21,210
Six months of sunlight. Six months of night.

112
00:07:21,451 --> 00:07:24,122
How does anyone survive a six-month night?

113
00:07:24,122 --> 00:07:28,868
On the day side, the ground bakes for months, with no night to cool off.

114
00:07:28,868 --> 00:07:33,037
On the night side, it gets colder than any Antarctic winter.

115
00:07:33,037 --> 00:07:38,144
Most plants need sunlight every day. Six months of darkness kills them.

116
00:07:38,394 --> 00:07:43,456
Meanwhile, the oceans have finished moving. One huge ocean at each pole.

117
00:07:43,456 --> 00:07:48,052
And around the middle, one giant continent wrapped around the whole planet.

118
00:07:48,052 --> 00:07:54,321
Even the air thins out at the equator. Breathing there would feel like climbing a high mountain.

119
00:07:54,321 --> 00:07:58,458
So where can you live? Here. The thin line between day and night.

120
00:07:58,458 --> 00:08:02,005
An endless sunset. Not too hot. Not too cold.

121
00:08:02,234 --> 00:08:05,167
But there's a catch. The line is moving.

122
00:08:05,167 --> 00:08:09,250
At the equator, it moves about 4.6 km/h.

123
00:08:09,250 --> 00:08:11,385
That's walking speed.

124
00:08:11,483 --> 00:08:16,324
To live in a forever-sunset, you just… keep walking. East. Forever.

125
00:08:16,421 --> 00:08:17,582
Every day?

126
00:08:17,582 --> 00:08:20,011
There are no days anymore.

127
00:08:20,757 --> 00:08:24,733
And while we walk… something invisible above us may start to change.

128
00:08:24,733 --> 00:08:26,751
Earth has a shield.

129
00:08:26,751 --> 00:08:29,239
And it might need the spin.

130
00:08:29,265 --> 00:08:34,777
Animals would have to adapt. Follow the sunset. Sleep through the night. Or dig.

131
00:08:34,777 --> 00:08:38,084
Sleep through a six-month night? …Okay, that part I like.

132
00:08:38,084 --> 00:08:41,163
Let's jump much further. A thousand years.

133
00:08:41,163 --> 00:08:43,439
Do I want to see what the map looks like?

134
00:08:43,439 --> 00:08:45,239
You really do.

135
00:08:46,358 --> 00:08:48,614
Welcome to the new Earth.

136
00:08:48,614 --> 00:08:52,867
Most of the old oceans near the equator are dry land now.

137
00:08:56,323 --> 00:08:59,168
One giant equatorial continent.

138
00:08:59,518 --> 00:09:01,616
And two polar seas.

139
00:09:02,766 --> 00:09:09,177
And the planet itself is changing shape. Deep down, rock flows, very slowly, like thick honey.

140
00:09:09,177 --> 00:09:12,347
The fat middle sinks, and Earth gets rounder.

141
00:09:12,347 --> 00:09:16,073
That means earthquakes. Cracks. Shifting coastlines.

142
00:09:16,073 --> 00:09:22,606
Any humans left would live underground, or near the polar seas, or on the march with the sunset.

143
00:09:22,606 --> 00:09:28,057
Now, the shield. Earth's magnetic field comes from liquid iron swirling in the core.

144
00:09:28,057 --> 00:09:30,969
And the spin helps organise that swirl.

145
00:09:30,969 --> 00:09:34,494
Without it, many scientists think the field could weaken.

146
00:09:34,494 --> 00:09:36,923
But nobody knows for sure.

147
00:09:36,988 --> 00:09:43,983
Look at Venus. It spins so slowly, its day is longer than its year. And it has no magnetic field.

148
00:09:44,133 --> 00:09:47,514
But Mercury also spins slowly… and it still has one.

149
00:09:47,514 --> 00:09:48,950
So the answer is…?

150
00:09:48,950 --> 00:09:52,305
The answer is "scientists are still arguing."

151
00:09:52,305 --> 00:10:02,264
If the shield did fade, more space radiation would reach the ground. Over millions of years, the solar wind could strip away our air. Like it did on Mars.

152
00:10:02,375 --> 00:10:05,409
Which brings me to an uncomfortable question.

153
00:10:05,409 --> 00:10:08,074
Is Earth already slowing down?

154
00:10:10,458 --> 00:10:12,258
Yes.

155
00:10:13,460 --> 00:10:17,412
Slowly. The Moon has been braking us for billions of years.

156
00:10:17,412 --> 00:10:22,711
1.4 billion years ago, a day lasted only about 19 hours.

157
00:10:22,861 --> 00:10:27,193
And the Moon is drifting away. 3.8 centimetres a year.

158
00:10:27,193 --> 00:10:32,684
We measure it with lasers, bounced off mirrors the Apollo astronauts left there.

159
00:10:32,684 --> 00:10:35,468
Which brings us back to my promise.

160
00:10:35,468 --> 00:10:39,727
The one thing in your night sky that would start this planet spinning again…

161
00:10:39,727 --> 00:10:41,527
…it's the Moon.

162
00:10:42,642 --> 00:10:47,109
Even on a stopped Earth, the Moon still pulls tides across the planet.

163
00:10:47,109 --> 00:10:51,833
Those tides drag. Slowly. Like a hand pushing a merry-go-round.

164
00:10:51,983 --> 00:10:59,579
Over tens or hundreds of millions of years, the Moon would turn the Earth again. About one spin every 27 days.

165
00:10:59,579 --> 00:11:01,538
The Moon saves us!

166
00:11:01,685 --> 00:11:03,513
But nothing is free.

167
00:11:03,513 --> 00:11:07,074
To spin us up, the Moon gives away its own energy.

168
00:11:07,074 --> 00:11:09,579
So instead of drifting away…

169
00:11:09,579 --> 00:11:12,185
…it starts falling toward us.

170
00:11:13,834 --> 00:11:21,664
It's already happening somewhere. Mars's moon Phobos is spiralling inward right now. One day, it will be torn apart.

171
00:11:21,914 --> 00:11:26,709
That's the cost. To save Earth's spin, the Moon slowly falls toward us.

172
00:11:26,709 --> 00:11:30,138
Okay. I'm awake. I'm SO awake. Put it back.

173
00:11:30,715 --> 00:11:32,826
Wait. If the Moon is that important…

174
00:11:32,826 --> 00:11:34,585
What if we just… lost the Moon?

175
00:11:36,825 --> 00:11:38,601
That's worse.

176
00:11:38,601 --> 00:11:40,913
Much worse. That's next.

177
00:11:42,466 --> 00:11:43,769
…Five more minutes?

178
00:11:43,769 --> 00:11:45,569
Nope.
